# `pass` is a statement, so it cannot be a value if/match branch's tail -- every
# branch of one must produce a value. The message names the fix rather than
# reporting "unknown variable 'pass'", which is what the bare identifier would
# otherwise resolve to.
#
# Checked in the RESOLVER, not the parser: only the resolver knows whether `pass`
# is a declared variable here. A parser-side guard was written first and refused
# a legitimate `pass` variable read as a branch tail (tests/pass_stmt.ty has one).
#
#   error: `pass` is a statement and produces no value
fn main():
    x := if true:
        pass
    else:
        1
    println(str(x))
